The C is a CAF header parser with several quirks that had to be preserved rather than fixed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstruct caf_chunk[0m is [38;5;10m{u32 type; s64 size;}[0m, so ABI padding puts [38;5;10msize[0m at offset 8 and makes the header 16 bytes — not the 12-byte header real CAF uses. The chunk walk is reproduced with those offsets. I verified all layouts by compiling an [38;5;10moffsetof[0m probe against the real header, and locked them in with [38;5;10mconst _: () = assert!(...)[0m on [38;5;10moffset_of![0m/[38;5;10msize_of[0m so a layout drift is a compile error.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mima_btoh*[0m are unconditional byte swaps, i.e. the code assumes a little-endian host. Kept as-is.[0m[0m
- The sample rate goes through [38;5;10mconv64.u = desc->sample_rate[0m, which is a [3mnumeric[23m [38;5;10mdouble[0m→[38;5;10mu64[0m truncation, not the bit reinterpretation the union suggests. The truncated integer is then byte-swapped and read back as a [38;5;10mdouble[0m. For any normal big-endian sample rate this yields [38;5;10m0.0[0m, and that is what both libraries produce.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdesc[0m/[38;5;10mpakt[0m can still be null after the loop and are dereferenced unconditionally; the loop also has no bounds check and no termination other than finding a [38;5;10mdata[0m chunk. Reproduced.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One thing worth flagging: that [38;5;10mdouble[0m→[38;5;10mu64[0m conversion is undefined behavior in C whenever the operand is negative, NaN, or ≥ 2^64, which is reachable from crafted input. I matched gcc's x86-64 codegen exactly ([38;5;10mcomisd[0m against 2^63, then [38;5;10msubsd[0m/[38;5;10mcvttsd2si[0m/[38;5;10mbtc[0m, with NaN falling through the [38;5;10mjnb[0m), since [38;5;10mcc[0m here resolves to gcc and that is what the CMake build would use. clang chooses a different sequence for those out-of-range cases, so C-vs-Rust output diverges for such inputs if the reference is built with clang — the underlying C has no defined answer there. Inputs with ordinary sample rates agree under both compilers.[0m[0m
